From 05319b45414747b5d52cda1bf609c39d23b6a9ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 19:31:55 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 39/40] dos: feed the audio device its own format -- the
 per-chunk conversion  becomes a memcpy

Round 1 of the 486DX2-50 campaign (patch 0038's message has the full
measurement) found audio conversion taking 43% of every frame on the
port's minimum-target CPU: 4.2 chunks of 23 ms audio per 98.8 ms frame,
each chunk ~10 ms of CPU, against 54 ms of actual game work. The low
tier, which converts 40% as much per chunk, measured 14.79 fps in the
same round; the high tier about 10 fps. The device on that rig -- a
PicoGUS in Sound Blaster 2.0 mode, DSP 2.x, 8-bit DMA -- plays U8 mono
at 22050 Hz whichever tier is built. So the high tier's stereo channel
and 16-bit depth were being produced by the game's callback (a double
multiply, two double compares and a float-to-int truncation per sample,
plus a heap allocation per call) and then thrown away by SDL's per-chunk
ConvertAudio() (16-bit to float, stereo to mono, float to 8-bit), on
every one of the ~43 chunks a second.

Two changes, both in startMusic()/audioCallback():

1. The stream opens in the WAV's own format as SDL_LoadWAV reported it,
   and the device-rate request follows the file, instead of both coming
   from the compile-time tier constants while songSpec was ignored. That
   ignored spec is how the committed low-tier SONG.WAV played at 4x speed
   under every high-tier benchmark build (see the Makefile's stage-time
   tier guard for the build-side half of that fix); the runtime is now
   correct for any file.

2. Once the device is open, the song is converted ONCE into the format
   the device actually negotiated (SDL_GetAudioDeviceFormat), with
   integer math -- S16 mono/stereo to U8 mono/stereo, and the S16
   mono/stereo pair either way, at equal rates; anything else is left
   as loaded and SDL converts per chunk exactly as before -- and
   SDL_SetAudioStreamFormat() tells the stream its input is now that
   format. ConvertAudio() then takes its memcpy path on both sides. The
   original 12 MB high-tier buffer becomes a 3 MB U8 mono one on that
   hardware; a real SB16 negotiates S16 stereo, which the high-tier song
   already is, so it takes the same fast path with no conversion at all.

The callback hands SDL the song's own bytes at unity gain (at most two
puts, at the loop seam), which is the whole of a life except the title
screen and the death fade; those use integer gain in the song's own
format through one reused scratch buffer. No per-call allocation.

A witness line ("DOS-PORT audio: song ... -> device ...") goes to stdout
at startMusic so a run log records which formats met and whether the
conversion happened.

Verified three ways before any rig time:
- Host unit test against SDL's own SDL_ConvertAudioSamples() on the real
  songs: S16 stereo -> U8 mono (the PicoGUS path), -> U8 stereo, -> S16
  mono, and S16 mono -> U8 mono all agree to within 1 LSB over every
  sample (max |diff| 1, mean 0.24-0.48; the difference is truncation vs
  SDL's float rounding); S16 mono -> S16 stereo is exact; the gain path
  and the unity-gain copy across the loop seam are exact.
- DOSBox-X, three full natural lives: emulated SB16 (song S16 2ch ->
  device S16 2ch, no conversion) 14.97 fps; low tier (S16 1ch -> S16
  2ch) 15.02 fps; emulated Sound Blaster 2.0 -- the rig's path -- song
  S16 2ch 22050 -> device U8 1ch 22050, 11995200 bytes -> 2998800, 15.02
  fps, fps_p50 15.00 / fps_p95 14.99. Correctness only, never a
  performance number.
- The witness line prints on every launch as designed.

